### Why does lazy initialization matter?

Lazy initialization means not building the instance until first use. This matters when construction is costly, such as loading a large audit configuration, and the instance might not be needed in every run.

### How does eager initialization work?

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class AuditLedger {
    private static final AuditLedger INSTANCE = new AuditLedger();

    private AuditLedger() {
        System.out.println("AuditLedger loaded");
    }

    public static AuditLedger getInstance() {
        return INSTANCE;
    }

    public void log(String entry) {
        System.out.println("AUDIT: " + entry);
    }
}
```

Thread-safe because the JVM initialises a class once under a lock. Not lazy: the instance is created at class load.

### How does the synchronized getter work?

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class AuditLedgerSync {
    private static AuditLedgerSync instance;

    private AuditLedgerSync() {}

    public static synchronized AuditLedgerSync getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new AuditLedgerSync();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Lazy and correct, but every call locks, even after the instance exists. Under heavy concurrent reads, this is a bottleneck.

### How does double-checked locking work?

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class AuditLedgerDCL {
    private static volatile AuditLedgerDCL instance;

    private AuditLedgerDCL() {}

    public static AuditLedgerDCL getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {                        // first check
            synchronized (AuditLedgerDCL.class) {
                if (instance == null) {                // second check
                    instance = new AuditLedgerDCL();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

The `volatile` keyword is essential. Without it, `instance = new AuditLedgerDCL()` is not atomic: the JVM may allocate memory, assign the reference, then construct the object. Another thread sees a non-null reference pointing to a partially-constructed object. With `volatile`, this reordering is forbidden.

### How does the Bill Pugh holder work?

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class AuditLedgerBillPugh {

    private AuditLedgerBillPugh() {
        System.out.println("AuditLedger created (Bill Pugh)");
    }

    private static class Holder {
        static final AuditLedgerBillPugh INSTANCE = new AuditLedgerBillPugh();
    }

    public static AuditLedgerBillPugh getInstance() {
        return Holder.INSTANCE;
    }
}
```

The outer class loading does not initialise the holder. The holder is loaded only when `getInstance()` first references `Holder.INSTANCE`. Because the JVM performs class initialization exactly once under its own lock, this is both lazy and thread-safe with no `volatile` and no `synchronized`.

### How does the enum singleton work?

```java
package com.bank.config;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;

public enum RateRegistry {
    INSTANCE;

    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> rates = Map.of(
        "SAVINGS", new BigDecimal("0.04"),
        "CURRENT", new BigDecimal("0.02")
    );

    public BigDecimal getRate(String type) {
        return rates.getOrDefault(type, BigDecimal.ZERO);
    }
}
```

The JVM guarantees a single instance. Enums are safe against serialization, reflection, and cloning. Joshua Bloch calls this the best way. Its only limitation: an enum cannot extend a class.

### How do you defend against serialization, reflection, and cloning?

```java
package com.bank.config;

import java.io.Serializable;

public class SecureAuditLedger implements Serializable {
    private static final SecureAuditLedger INSTANCE = new SecureAuditLedger();

    private SecureAuditLedger() {
        if (INSTANCE != null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Use getInstance()");
        }
    }

    public static SecureAuditLedger getInstance() { return INSTANCE; }

    private Object readResolve() { return INSTANCE; }

    @Override
    protected Object clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {
        throw new CloneNotSupportedException("Singleton");
    }
}
```

The enum eliminates all three guards for free.

### Which approach should you choose?

| Approach | Lazy | Thread-safe | Serialization-safe | Reflection-safe |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Eager | No | Yes | No | No |
| Synchronized | Yes | Yes | No | No |
| DCL (volatile) | Yes | Yes | No | No |
| Bill Pugh | Yes | Yes | No (add readResolve) | No (add guard) |
| Enum | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- A JVM Singleton is one per JVM, not one per cluster. Across a cluster, use a shared store.
- In Spring, beans are singletons by default via the container scope, not via the Singleton pattern.
- Singletons carry global state and make unit testing harder. Prefer DI in production.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SingletonLazyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Enum
        BigDecimal rate = RateRegistry.INSTANCE.getRate("SAVINGS");
        System.out.println("Savings rate: " + rate);

        // Bill Pugh
        AuditLedgerBillPugh ledger = AuditLedgerBillPugh.getInstance();
        ledger.log("Transfer TXN-001 completed");
    }
}
```

```text
Savings rate: 0.04
AuditLedger created (Bill Pugh)
AUDIT: Transfer TXN-001 completed
```
